//? Title: Fibonacci Search: Actual Schedules, Reads and Fallbacks
//? Subtitle: Exact-match array search with checked offsets, owned transitions and separate comparison counts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Find an integer in a nondecreasing array and report its position or absence.
Fibonacci search narrows its schedule with consecutive Fibonacci sizes rather
than computing midpoint divisions. We begin with two actual reads on seven
values, retain the original ten-value array and five queries, and inspect the
native solver's pre/post states. The report-local retracing loop is replaced
by same-pass owned records. Repeated clamped positions, an equality fallback,
duplicates and a full query census distinguish reads from unique positions,
primitive comparisons and elapsed time. A pure native schedule API checks
very large hypothetical lengths without allocating corresponding arrays.
)

\toc

> Two actual reads find nine

In [1,3,5,7,9,11,13], the smallest covering Fibonacci size is eight.
Its predecessors are five and three. The native implementation starts with
offset−1, probes offset+small=2 and reads five. Since five is below nine,
it moves the offset to two and reduces the schedule from(3,5,8) to(2,3,5).
The next probe is index four, whose value nine matches. There are two reads,
three primitive key comparisons and one nonmatching schedule transition.

The diagram highlights only native captured reads. The boxes below the
array show each actual pre/post trio; no local search loop recreates them.
The result is an index in the original sorted array, unlike the separate
Eytzinger layout's index convention.

