> The native schedule and its shrinking invariant

For positive n, grow(small,middle,large) from(0,1,1) until large≥n,
maintaining small+middle=large. The current offset is initially−1.
During the main loop, probe min(offset+small,n−1). A read value below
the key moves the offset to that index and updates the sizes as
(new small,new middle,new large)=(old middle−old small,old small,old middle).
A value above the key keeps the offset and sets
(new small,new middle,new large)=(2old small−old middle,old middle−old small,old small).
The implementation uses successive nonnegative differences, avoiding the
unnecessary doubled intermediate in that second expression.

Nondecreasing order justifies discarding the corresponding values. Each
nonmatching main iteration decreases the Fibonacci rank by at least one.
When large≤1, a possible remaining offset+1 position is checked by equality.
That final check is a real read even when a previous read visited the same
position. Covering-rank growth and search each take O(log(n+1)) word work.
The schedule-addition count plus two is a conservative read bound, not an
exact count or a claim of optimal comparisons.

There is no midpoint division in this schedule. That arithmetic property
alone does not imply faster execution than binary search on current
hardware. Index checks, branches, key comparisons, memory access and
preprocessing all matter. Fibonacci search here is exact membership in
an already sorted array, distinct from unimodal numerical optimization.

> The original ten values and five queries

For n=10, the initial trio is(5,8,13). The original search for thirteen
reads index:value pairs4:9,7:15,5:11,6:13. All five original results and
probe counts are preserved. The binary-search comparison below calls the
native closed-interval binary-search trace; on this particular query its
four positions happen to coincide. The next section shows paths with
different behavior. No equivalence of their general search trees is claimed.

