> Repeated clamps, the equality fallback and duplicates

The native implementation intentionally retains its original index choices
and probe counts. For absent key twenty, indices4,7,9,9,9 are all main-loop
reads. Clamping repeats the last position three times, making five reads
but only three distinct positions. For absent key eight, the path is
4,1,2,3,4: the fifth read is the final equality-only fallback. The first
and last read inspect the same position. That fallback executes one
comparison; it does not invent an ordering test for its explanatory record.

For duplicates, a matching index is the first one encountered by this
search schedule. It need not be the first sorted occurrence. An eight-item
constant array returns index two for its repeated key. Empty input returns
false,index−1 and zero work; singleton lookup uses the equality fallback.
Sortedness remains an unchecked precondition, so these statements require
a nondecreasing input that stays unmodified during the search.

