\caption(The native pure schedule reaches the full signed-index length domain with no array allocation. Separate limits verify main and fallback capture admission and ownership.)
\alttext(A native three-box diagram shows the exact small,middle and covering Fibonacci sizes for the maximum signed-index length. The caption states the actual schedule-addition count and that no input array was allocated.)

> Verification and references

The original callable signature and valid result/index/probe behavior are
preserved across1157680 frozen lines, including all lengths0..1024 and
queries,1000 seeded duplicate arrays and integer extremes. All ten original
full-program output lines also remain exact. Strict sanitizer checks cover
independent standard membership, all165 small nondecreasing arrays over
three values, all lengths0..4096 at edge queries,600 seeded wide/duplicate
arrays,10000 virtual full-domain schedules checked with wider integers,
a million-element input, actual pre/post records, fallback/repeated reads,
quotas and ownership. All16 examples in the changed header, including its
existing Eytzinger examples, are compiled and executed.

Paul E. Black, [Fibonaccian search](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/fibonaccianSearch.html),
NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures, entry modified14December2020,
describes the Fibonacci-sized sorted-array search family and its subtraction
scheduling. The native variant starts with the smaller predecessor offset,
clamps positions and keeps a final equality fallback; the exact contract and
actual counts here describe that implementation.

P. S. Efraimidis, [(alpha,beta) Fibonacci Search](https://arxiv.org/abs/1012.0259),
2010, introduction, discusses historical arithmetic motivation and explicitly
distinguishes sorted-array search from unimodal optimization. Its generalized
asymmetric-cost optimum is a different model; this report claims no such
optimality for the native exact-match/clamped variant.
